You can now expose your agents, workflows, and tools to MCP using the new[built-in MCP server](https://mastra.ai/docs/server/mcp) . Install` @mastra/mcp` , configure auth, and your whole Mastra server becomes available at` /api/mcp` .


The MCP server comes equipped with tools to` call_agent` ,` start_workflow` , and` get_trace` . Configured coding agents, or connected MCP clients, can then handle tasks you'd normally do yourself using the Studio UI.


Your browser does not support the video tag.


This release builds on our existing[MCP 2.0](https://mastra.ai/blog/introducing-mcp-2) support, and joins[MCP tasks](https://mastra.ai/blog/introducing-mcp-tasks) and[MCP events](https://mastra.ai/blog/introducing-mcp-events) as the latest addition to Mastra's MCP server and client capabilities.


Requests will typically require a bearer token, verified by your auth provider. Each MCP tool call uses the same server routes as standard HTTP requests, and goes through the same[RBAC](https://mastra.ai/docs/studio/auth#role-based-access-control) and[FGA](https://mastra.ai/docs/auth/fga) checks (if configured). You can also choose which agents, workflows, and tools the MCP server exposes.


## Get started


Install the following packages, this example uses Mastra platform auth. Any supported[auth provider](https://mastra.ai/docs/auth/overview#third-party-integrations) will also work:


Terminal


```text
npm   install   @mastra/core@latest   @mastra/mcp@latest   @mastra/auth-studio@latest
```


note


Requires` @mastra/core@1.83.0` or later, added in[PR #29929](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/pull/29929) .


## Configure auth


Configure an auth provider on your main Mastra instance to expose the MCP server at` <server-url>/api/mcp` :


src/mastra/index.ts


```text
import   {   MastraAuthStudio   }   from   "  @mastra/auth-studio  "  ;


export   const   mastra   =   new   Mastra  ({
// ...
server: {
auth:   new   MastraAuthStudio  ({
organizationId: process.env.MASTRA_ORGANIZATION_ID
})
}
});
```


In production, if an auth provider isn't configured, Mastra won't expose the MCP server, but you can override this using` enabled: true` :


src/mastra/index.ts


```text
export   const   mastra   =   new   Mastra  ({
// ...
server: {
mcp: {
enabled:   true
}
}
});
```


## Choose what to expose


For tighter control over what to expose, use` toolsets` ,` include` , and` exclude` to choose specific agents, workflows, or tools:


src/mastra/index.ts


```text
export   const   mastra   =   new   Mastra  ({
// ...
server: {
mcp: {
toolsets: [  "  agents  "  ,   "  tools  "  ],
include: { agents: [  "  support-agent  "  ,   "  billing-agent  "  ] },
exclude: { tools: [  "  delete-account  "  ] }
}
}
});
```


## Connect Claude Code


Once you've configured and deployed your Mastra project, you can connect it to Claude Code using` mcp add` , followed by the server URL and an authorization token:


Terminal


```text
claude   mcp   add   --transport   http   sit-rep   <  server-ur  l  >  /api/mcp   --header   "  Authorization: Bearer <token>  "
```


## Chat to your Mastra server


After you've added the MCP server to Claude Code's config, you can call your agents, run workflows and tools, and read memory or traces:


```text
Run the docs-sync-workflow
```


